# <NAME> — AGENTS.md

Jekyll + minima Markdown site. claude-hub conventions:

- **Local preview** runs via `jekyll@<NAME>.service` (systemd, `Restart=always`)
  → `serve-local.sh`, which runs `bundle exec jekyll serve --baseurl /<NAME>` on
  port `<PORT>`. Don't run `jekyll serve` by hand — the unit owns the port.
- **Reverse-proxied** at `/<NAME>/` by claude-hub (`stripPrefix: false`, so the
  `/<NAME>` baseurl is required — already wired into `serve-local.sh`).
- **Card metadata** comes from `README.md`: H1 = title, first paragraph =
  description, frontmatter `tags: [...]` = badges. `README.md` is also the site
  index (`permalink: /`).
- **`Gemfile.local`, not `Gemfile`**: GitHub Pages' build looks for a file named
  literally `Gemfile`, so `Gemfile.local` is invisible to the deploy and only
  drives the local preview. Bundler installs into a project-local
  `vendor/bundle` (see `.bundle/config`).

## What to do first

1. Ask the user what to build.
2. Update `README.md` (H1, first paragraph, tags).
3. Add Markdown pages. Set the site `title` / `description` / `theme` in
   `_config.yml`.

## Deploy (GitHub Pages)

Pages builds this natively with its own `github-pages` gem (ignores
`Gemfile.local`). For a project Pages site served under a subpath, set
`baseurl: "/<repo>"` in `_config.yml` — the local preview gets its baseurl from
`serve-local.sh` instead, so the two don't fight.

## Restarting the preview

```bash
sudo systemctl restart jekyll@<NAME>.service
journalctl -u jekyll@<NAME>.service -f
```
